Z||000005||rockcrawl||08-03-2000||05:31 PM||||I've been told the TH425 is much stronger than the TH325-4L. Unfortunately it's only a 3 speed, but for a drag car that would be fine. I think the 325 could handle just about any stock V8, but if you're building a monster rat motor, it may not hold up. A big block should work just as well because there's plenty of room, but of course it will just add more weight. I think the trans would be equally strong in standard or reverse, so it's just a matter of weight distribution. I don't know the actual #s, but the engine is about centered over the axles, so reversing it puts almost the entire weight of the trans in front of the axle, instead of behind it. It is a heavy trans, so I'm sure it would make a noticeable difference. The engine also sits a few inches off to the right (passenger)side, if reversed it would be to the left. For the drag car, why not put a big block in the front like this one-- <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/bigblockfiero2.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/bigblockfiero2.jpg</A>  I took that picture at Maple Grove Raceway a few years ago. Owner is unknown. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
