Z||000007||mrfixit58||08-04-2000||11:51 AM||||Rockcrawl,<P>I re-read your write-up on the TH325 install and had a couple of questions about the reverse rotation:<P>- When you say "turn the transmission 180 degrees", I assume you don't mean to flip the transmission, do you?<P>- If you do just turn the transmission so the bell housing faces the rear, how does this lower the motor?<P>Like you, I'm just trying to get details for a possible future project. I've got a TPI 350 motor that's just itch'n to jump into a Fiero.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||08-04-2000||12:56 PM||||sorry, double post<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 08-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||rockcrawl||08-04-2000||01:04 PM||||HYPTNOTiSE, I don't know if anyone really offers a kit to do it. I think Ryane Motorsports makes a tubular cradle with all the correct mounts for the trans and whatever engine you want. Mine cost me next to nothing because I was able to do 95% of the work myself. I paid $200 for the trans, $300 for a 7000 mile 350, $300 for the TPI setup, $80 for axle plates, plus various hoses, clamps, etc.  <P>Roy, Normally the engine pulleys are at the front of the car. By rotating the engine/trans 180 deg, I mean turn it around so the pulleys are at the rear (in the trunk). Without any other changes, this would give you 4 reverse speeds, and one forward. The differential housing bolts on to the trans, and it must also be rotated upside down. Doing so will reverse the rotation of the axles because it puts the pinion gear on the opposite side of the ring gear. Also, the pinion shaft enters the differential at an angle parallel and perpendicular to the axle shafts, but on a higher plane, so rotating it will put it lower, esentially lowering the entire engine/trans in relation to the axle shafts. Sorry if I've lost you there, You almost have to see how the trans is made to understand it. It's totally different than a Fiero trans. The trans basically has a single output that is parallel to the engines crankshaft. This is connected to a cast iron differential housing that looks very much like a rear axle on an older rear wheel drive car or truck, but it just has really short axles (also like the center section on a Vette IRS). <P>I've never done a reverse swap, I think it was the guy at Ryane that told me about it.<P>Hope that info helps,<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||08-05-2000||10:41 AM||||Rockcrawl,<P>Not actually having taken a TH325 apart, it's difficult to understand just how all the components fit together. I understood the part about rotating the "pumpkin" but I'm still having difficulties with: "the pinion shaft enters the differential at an angle parallel and perpendicular to the axle shafts, but on a higher plane, so rotating it will put it lower, esentially lowering the entire engine/trans in relation to the axle shafts". If you have pictures of the components it would greatly help.<P>As far as the rever rotation of the motor, I assume it would require a different cam, water pump, and starter. I don't know who carries these components but was thinking that maybe, since they often use two motors to drive two different propellers, that a marine division would be a good place to look.<P>Any thoughts?<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000011||rockcrawl||08-05-2000||09:38 PM||||mrfixit58,<BR> I guess my description wasn't quite clear. I'm trying to find a picture of the trans, That will help greatly. The engine rotation doesn't get reversed. Flipping the pumpkin upside down is what reverses the direction of drive. Now that I think about it, I'm not sure if the pumpkin has a high pinion or a low pinion. I don't have mine anymore to check. But anyway, rotating it will affect engine height relative to axle location.<BR> <BR>&gt;As far as the rever rotation of the motor, &gt;I assume it would require a different cam, &gt;water pump, and starter.<BR>That's correct, but not neccesary for this conversion. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
